# What this app calls itself and what it wears: the browser tab, the top bar and
# the sign-in screen. The logo is a file in public/ (logo.svg is the one shipped
# — replace that file, or point this at another name); an absolute URL works too.
# The tab icon lives in index.html: the head is parsed before any of this is
# read, so it is edited on disk instead.
VITE_APP_NAME=
VITE_APP_LOGO=

# Chatfuel dashboard token — generate it at https://panel.chatfuel.com/integration/auth/token
# Read ONLY by the proxy (dev server and prod server) — never exposed to the browser.
CHATFUEL_TOKEN=

# The Chatfuel workspace the app opens on (the wizard fills it in). Not a fence:
# every workspace the token's account owns is in the topbar picker, and the bots
# inside them are read live, so one created later needs no rebuild. With the auth
# module the app opens nothing of its own and this is unused.
VITE_CHATFUEL_WORKSPACE_ID=

# Upstream API base (change only for staging environments).
CHATFUEL_API_BASE=https://panel.chatfuel.com

# Where the app sends somebody to do what it deliberately does not do — create a
# workspace, create a bot, look at the bill. The browser-side half of
# CHATFUEL_API_BASE, which is unprefixed and so unreadable from the bundle;
# unset, the empty states say panel.chatfuel.com. Set it on a deployment
# pointed at another Chatfuel, or those two states name an address that is a
# dead end there.
# VITE_CHATFUEL_DASHBOARD_URL=

# --- Auth (the "auth" module) --------------------------------------------
# All three together turn the proxy's auth gate ON: every request must carry
# a Supabase session whose user is a member of this tenant. None of them =
# open mode. Some but not all = the proxy fails closed (ProxyAuthMisconfigured).
# VITE_* are baked into the client at build time AND read by the proxy at
# runtime — keep build and runtime env identical.
# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Supabase secret (service-role) key. Server-side only, and NOT optional with
# the auth module: it is what registers a bot the server just created (at sign-up
# and every time somebody adds another), and it mounts POST
# /chatfuel/auth/recovery-link so admins can issue password-reset links before
# custom SMTP is configured.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Lets that route deliver. Its ONLY delivery channel is the server log, and the
# link it writes there is a working password reset for the account it names —
# readable by every member of the Vercel project, anyone with shell access on
# your own server, anyone who can run `docker logs`. That is a wider set of
# people than the owners and admins the route admits, so the write is opt-in:
# unset, the route mints nothing and answers 501. Configure SMTP in Supabase and
# you never need this.
#
# Turning it on does not widen WHO may be named. That is the database's decision
# (cf_recovery_authorize), and it is narrower than "a member of my workspace":
# the target must also rank strictly below the caller and belong to no other
# workspace on this deployment — a recovery link resets an account, not a
# membership, so a target who stands in a second workspace would carry this
# workspace's admin into one they were never admitted to. Every issue leaves a
# row in cf_recovery_events, because a log is not a record of who asked.
# AUTH_RECOVERY_LINK_LOG=

# The Chatfuel workspace EVERY bot of EVERY account is created in — the one whose
# plan pays for them all, so its bot limit is the ceiling for the whole
# deployment, not per customer. Server-side only; not a secret. Without it the
# server refuses to create bots rather than putting them where nobody pays for
# them. Only the auth module reads it; it is unrelated to
# VITE_CHATFUEL_WORKSPACE_ID above.
# CHATFUEL_WORKSPACE_ID=

# --- Scheduled posts (the "publishing" module) ---------------------------
# What the app and its database use to prove a request came from the other. The
# database holds only its sha256 (put there by the migration): it sends that hash
# back as the callback's credential, and the app proves itself in the other
# direction with the value below. Server-side only, and needed only for posts
# that go out unattended — without it the app still composes and publishes on the
# spot, it just offers no schedule.
# PUBLISHING_SECRET=

# The storage bucket the composer's uploads live in. The APP and the DATABASE
# must name the same one — the app builds the public URL it hands Instagram, and
# a bucket only the migration knows about is a 400 at publish time — so change
# it in the migration too, or let the wizard fill both from this value
# (PUBLISHING_MEDIA_BUCKET in the environment you run it with). Letters, digits,
# dot, dash and underscore; anything else falls back to the default.
PUBLISHING_MEDIA_BUCKET=cf-pub-media

# How much of that bucket ONE BOT may hold, in megabytes, and how long a file no
# post refers to any more survives, in days. The per-file ceiling is 25 MB and
# says nothing about how many files there are; these two say. An upload past the
# quota is refused with a 507, and a file that is both older than the TTL and
# named by no post is let go on the next upload — an abandoned compose is what
# leaves those behind. Defaults: 512 and 30.
# PUBLISHING_MEDIA_QUOTA_MB=512
# PUBLISHING_MEDIA_TTL_DAYS=30

# Set automatically on deployments whose production URL is protected. The app
# passes it to the database when scheduling is turned on, so the callback is not
# bounced at the edge before any code runs.
# VERCEL_AUTOMATION_BYPASS_SECRET=

# Where this deployment answers from the outside, e.g. https://posts.example.com
#
# REQUIRED to turn scheduling on, and to issue a password-recovery link. Both
# attach a credential to the address they name: the one recorded for scheduling
# is where the database posts a shared secret every minute, forever, and a
# recovery link is a working password reset for the account it names. Nothing in
# the request is allowed to decide that address — not the Host, not the Origin,
# not a body field — so a deployment that has not said which name it answers to
# is told to set this and nothing is written or issued. Local dev sets it to
# http://localhost:5173. Everything else in the app works without it.
# PUBLIC_URL=

# --- Admin panel (the "admin" module) ------------------------------------
# The password that opens /admin, and the only thing that does: the panel reads
# and changes the whole Chatfuel account behind CHATFUEL_TOKEN, so it is not
# gated by a Chatfuel or an app identity — an open deployment has no accounts at
# all, and in one that does the operator is usually not a customer of their own
# product. Server-side only; a VITE_ name would put it in the browser bundle.
#
# At least 16 characters, or the panel refuses to run and says why. That floor
# is not fussiness: the wrong-password counter lives in one process's memory,
# and a host that answers each request from a fresh instance gives every guess
# a fresh count. With the auth module the count is kept in your database as
# well (cf_admin_attempts), so instances share it; without one there is nowhere
# to keep it and the length of this value is the defence that survives.
#
# A session lasts two hours, and signing out is felt by the instance that
# served it and no other — so on a serverless host, changing this value is the
# only revocation that reaches every instance.
#
# The panel is never in the nav rail — /admin is the whole way in, so bookmark
# it. Unset = the admin routes are not mounted at all and /admin says there is
# no panel here. Changing it signs every open session out.
#
# Setting this on a host the internet can reach is a decision about your own
# Chatfuel account: this value is the only thing in front of it, and protecting
# the panel is yours to do. There is no second factor and no lockout that
# outlives a process. Use what a password manager generates, and see
# docs/deployment.md before you set it on a serverless host.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The salt the admin session cookie is signed under. Unset, it is derived from
# this deployment's own token and Supabase URL — stable across instances (so a
# host that answers from a fresh one each time still honours the cookie) and
# different per deployment. Set it to sign every open admin session out without
# changing the password, or when two deployments share a password and must not
# share cookies.
# ADMIN_COOKIE_SALT=

# Whether the x-forwarded-* headers may be believed. Off by default.
#
# On (`1` or `true`), the last hop of x-forwarded-for becomes the key the
# admin-login counter throttles on, and an x-forwarded-proto of https is enough
# to mark the admin cookie Secure. Turn it on ONLY behind an edge that writes
# those headers itself — Vercel, or a reverse proxy you run. With nothing in
# front, the headers are the caller's own text: a fresh forwarded address per
# attempt is a fresh throttle bucket per attempt, which is the rate limit gone.
#
# Off, the counter keys on the socket address — one bucket for everybody behind
# an edge, so the longest wait is capped lower there to keep one caller from
# shutting the door on the admin — and the cookie is Secure everywhere but
# loopback.
# TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=1

# Port for the production server (npm start). Default 3000.
# PORT=3000

# Interface npm start binds. Default 0.0.0.0 — every interface. 127.0.0.1 makes
# the deployment this machine's own, which is what open mode (no auth gate) may
# run on without asking for anything else.
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- Limits and who may call the proxy -----------------------------------
# Origins besides this app's own that may reach the proxy from a browser,
# comma- or space-separated ('*' allows every origin). The proxy forwards under
# CHATFUEL_TOKEN, so a request from a page you did not write is a request that
# page gets to make with your Chatfuel account: anything not listed here and not
# same-origin is answered 403 before the route runs, and a WebSocket upgrade is
# refused before a socket exists. Leave unset unless another origin serves your
# app.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# The names this deployment answers to, comma- or space-separated ('*' turns the
# check off). Different question from ALLOWED_ORIGINS: that one says which page
# may call, this one says whether the address that page used is one of yours. A
# name someone else owns, pointed at this deployment, arrives with an Origin and
# a Host that agree honestly — the origin check passes it, and only a list of
# your own names does not. Usually left unset: the hosts named by
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS and PUBLIC_URL are added for you, loopback is always allowed,
# and a deployment that named none of them and is not on loopback is not checked
# at all rather than guessed at. Set it for a tunnel or preview URL neither of
# those names (ngrok, a Cloudflare tunnel), and prefer naming that host to '*'.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Say, once, that this deployment is meant to answer strangers under one token.
# With no Supabase pair there is no auth gate, so a host anyone can reach is a
# master token anyone can drive — the server refuses to bind and the Vercel
# function answers 503 until you either install the auth module
# (npx @chatfuel/wizard --embed), bind loopback (HOST=127.0.0.1), or set this to
# 1. Not a fix, a signature: keep whatever authenticates in front of it there.
# It does not cover ALLOWED_ORIGINS='*' without a gate, which is refused either
# way.
# CHATFUEL_OPEN_PROXY=1

# How many /chatfuel/api/* uploads and GraphQL requests may be in flight at once
# (defaults 8 and 32) and how many browser WebSockets one process will hold
# (default 256). Each upload sits in memory while it is forwarded, each GraphQL
# request holds an upstream connection until Chatfuel answers, and each socket
# opens a second one upstream, so these are the ceilings on what a busy — or a
# hostile — client can cost the process. Past them: 503 ProxyBusy, and a refused
# upgrade. The GraphQL ceiling is the higher of the two because it is the route
# the whole app talks through.
# REST_MAX_CONCURRENT=8
# GRAPHQL_MAX_CONCURRENT=32
# WS_MAX_SOCKETS=256

# The same ceilings, per tenant rather than per process: how many requests one
# workspace may send per minute (429 TenantBusy past it) and how many sockets it
# may hold at once (closed 4429). Only felt with the gate on — without one there
# is a single tenant, and the ceilings above are already theirs.
# TENANT_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=600
# TENANT_MAX_SOCKETS=8

# Whether a request naming a flow, a block or a contact — rather than a bot — is
# checked against the bot that id was handed out under. The proxy learns which
# is which by watching its own answers. `bound` (the default with the gate on)
# refuses only an id it knows belongs to another workspace; `strict` also
# refuses one it has never seen; `off` is the default without the gate.
# CHATFUEL_RESOURCE_FENCE=bound

# Whether those bindings are shared through this deployment's own Supabase
# rather than living in one process's memory. One table, read when this process
# holds no answer and written only for the ids a caller actually names — which
# is a handful a session, not the thousands an answer carries. It is what makes
# `bound` survive a restart and `strict` safe on more than one instance. On by
# default with the gate and a service-role key. If Supabase does not answer the
# fence falls back to memory for a few seconds rather than making callers wait.
# CHATFUEL_RESOURCE_STORE=on

# Whether an operation this app does not send is forwarded at all. The list is
# generated from the shipped modules' own documents — 348 root fields out of a
# schema thousands wide — and anything else answers 403 OperationNotAllowed. On
# by default with the gate, off without it.
# CHATFUEL_OPERATION_ALLOWLIST=on
#
# The EXTRA list widens it by NAME, which says nothing about what the document
# under that name selects. An app that writes operations of its own is better
# served by adding them to src/operationDocs.ts, which is the document registry
# the proxy checks first: it forwards the documents this app ships and refuses
# every other one with 403 OperationNotInRegistry. There is no env var for it —
# it travels in the code, because it IS the code.
# CHATFUEL_OPERATION_ALLOWLIST_EXTRA=myOwnQuery,myOwnMutation
#
# Behind the gate, turning the fence off takes two variables rather than one:
# CHATFUEL_OPERATION_ALLOWLIST=off is ignored on its own, and the startup line
# says it was. Every caller behind the gate is a stranger, so `off` there is the
# master token answering the whole account schema — a thing to mean on purpose,
# not to inherit from a copied deploy script.
# CHATFUEL_OPERATION_ALLOWLIST_OFF=1

# --- Deploying -----------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run deploy` copies the values above into the Vercel project with
# `vercel env add --force` (**production only** — a preview build runs code
# nobody has reviewed yet while holding the master Chatfuel token and the
# Supabase service-role key, so the preview environment is left empty). Set
# DEPLOY_PREVIEW_ENV=1 to opt in and send them to preview as well; the price is
# that every branch push then deploys with those credentials in reach.
# This file itself is never uploaded, so the project environment is the only
# source there.
# CHATFUEL_TOKEN, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, PUBLISHING_SECRET, ADMIN_PASSWORD
# and ADMIN_COOKIE_SALT go up as sensitive (write-only);
# the rest stay readable in the dashboard.
# VITE_* are needed at BUILD time as well as runtime — they are baked into the
# client bundle — which is why they are project variables and not build args.
